.TH SHEDMAN-ROLLBACK 1 "2026-04-25" "ShedOS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shedman-rollback \- restore root subvolume from a snapper snapshot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo shedman rollback
.RB [ -y | --yes ]
.RB [ -k | --keep-current ]
.I N
.br
.B sudo shedman rollback
.RB { -l | --list }
.br
.B sudo shedman rollback
.RB { -u | --undo }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shedman rollback
restores the
.B @
btrfs subvolume from a snapper snapshot. Requires root. Reboot is
required for the rollback to take effect.
.PP
The model:
.IP 1. 4
The current
.B @
is renamed to
.BI @.rollback- <timestamp>
(safety net; nothing is deleted).
.IP 2. 4
A new
.B @
is snapshotted from the chosen snapper snapshot.
.IP 3. 4
On reboot, the new
.B @
is mounted at
.IR / .
.PP
.B @home,
.B @log,
.B @cache,
.B @pkg,
and the other data-carrying subvolumes are intentionally
.B not
rolled back. This is a system-state rollback only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I N
Snapper snapshot number to roll back to. Required unless
.B --list
or
.B --undo
is given.
.TP
.BR -l , " --list"
Show recent snapper snapshots and exit. Use this to find the right
.IR N .
.TP
.BR -u , " --undo"
Promote the most recent
.BI @.rollback- <ts>
backup back to
.BR @ .
Reboot to apply.
.TP
.BR -k , " --keep-current"
Pair with
.BR --undo :
keep the prior backup around even after the undo.
.TP
.BR -y , " --yes"
Skip the \(oqare you sure\(cq prompt.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /
The
.B @
subvolume.
.TP
.I /.snapshots/
Where snapper keeps its snapshots.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shedman (1),
.BR shedman-update (1),
.BR snapper (8).
